To create an editable table using JavaScript, you'll need to use a combination of HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. Here's a simple example that should get you started:

1. First, create the HTML structure of the table.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>Editable Table</title>
  <style>
    table, th, td {
      border: 1px solid black;
      padding: 5px;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <table id="editableTable">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Name</th>
        <th>Age</th>
        <th>City</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>John Doe</td>
        <td>30</td>
        <td>New York</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Jane Smith</td>
        <td>25</td>
        <td>Los Angeles</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <script src="editableTable.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

Here, we have defined a simple table with the ID `editableTable`. We have also added a link to the JavaScript file `editableTable.js`.

2. Next, create the `editableTable.js` file and write the JavaScript code to make the cells editable.

```javascript
document.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded", function () {
  let table = document.getElementById("editableTable");

  for (let row of table.rows) {
    for (let cell of row.cells) {
      cell.addEventListener("click", function () {
        let input = document.createElement("input");
        input.value = cell.innerText;

        cell.innerHTML = "";
        cell.appendChild(input);

        input.addEventListener("blur", function () {
          cell.innerHTML = input.value;
        });
      });
    }
  }
});
```

In this code, we add an event listener for the `DOMContentLoaded` event to ensure the DOM is fully loaded before running our script.

We then loop through each row and cell in the table, and add an event listener for the `click` event on each cell.

When a cell is clicked, we create an input element and set its value to the cell content. We then empty the cell and append the input element to the cell.

Lastly, we add an event listener for the `blur` event on the input element. When the input element loses focus, we set the cell's content to the input's value.

Now you have an editable table using JavaScript. Users can click on a cell to edit its content, and when they click outside the cell, the changes will be saved.

