    <table className="editable-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Name</th>
          <th>Age</th>
          <th>City</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {data.map((row, rowIndex) => (
          <tr key={rowIndex}>
            {Object.entries(row).map(([colName, colValue]) => (
              <td key={colName}>
                <input
                  type="text"
                  value={colValue}
                  onChange={e => editCell(e, rowIndex, colName)}
                />
              </td>
            ))}
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  );
};

const App = () => {
  return (
    <div className="App">
      <EditableTable />
    </div>
  );
}

export default App;
```

In this code, we create a functional component named `EditableTable`. We use the `useState` hook to manage the state of our data. We store the initial data in `initialData` and set it as the initial state using `setData`.

We then create a function called `editCell` which takes an event, rowIndex, and colName as arguments. This function updates the `data` state when an input changes.

In the JSX portion of the `EditableTable`, we map over the `data` and create rows in the HTML table. We also map over the entries in each row object and render input elements with the current value inside each cell. We add an `onChange` event listener to the input elements, which calls the `editCell` function when the input value changes.

