It looks like your application is stuck at `vkBeginCommandBuffer` because it is waiting for the frame with the same index to be available. Since the `inFlightFences` are used to keep track of the availability of each frame, this can happen if the fence associated to that frame is not signaled in time.

The problem seems to be that you update `currentFrame` only after successfully presenting the swapchain image (`vkQueuePresentKHR`). The `currentFrame` should actually be updated at the beginning of each iteration of your main application loop, regardless of whether the previous iteration succeeded in presenting the image or not.

Adjust your code to update `currentFrame` at the beginning of the main loop, similar to:

```cpp
// Main application loop
while (loopCondition) {
    currentFrame = (currentFrame + 1) % kMaxFramesInFlight;

    // Here, call your BeginFrame() and EndFrame() methods as needed
    renderer.BeginFrame();
    // Update and render objects
    renderer.EndFrame();

    // Other tasks
}
```

If this adjustment doesn't fix your issue, make sure your synchronization primitives (semaphores and fences) are properly set up and used during command buffer submission and presenting the swap chain image.

