Getting the motherboard ID is not as straightforward as getting the CPU ID, as there is no standard way to retrieve it. However, you can use the following code snippet in C++ on Visual Studio 2017 64-bit to attempt to retrieve the motherboard's serial number:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <Windows.h>

int main()
{
    HKEY hKey;
    LONG lResult = RegOpenKeyEx(HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE, "HARDWARE\\DESCRIPTION\\System\\BIOS", 0, KEY_READ, &hKey);
    if (lResult != ERROR_SUCCESS)
    {
        std::cout << "Error opening registry key." << std::endl;
        return 1;
    }

    char buffer[256];
    DWORD bufferSize = sizeof(buffer);
    DWORD type = REG_SZ;
    lResult = RegQueryValueEx(hKey, "BaseBoardSerialNumber", NULL, &type, (LPBYTE)buffer, &bufferSize);
    if (lResult != ERROR_SUCCESS)
    {
        std::cout << "Error getting motherboard serial number." << std::endl;
        RegCloseKey(hKey);
        return 1;
    }
    RegCloseKey(hKey);

    std::cout << "Motherboard ID: " << buffer << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This code relies on the `RegOpenKeyEx` and `RegQueryValueEx` functions from the Windows API to access the registry values for the motherboard. The code opens the `HARDWARE\\DESCRIPTION\\System\\BIOS` key in the registry and uses the `RegQueryValueEx` function to retrieve the value of the `BaseBoardSerialNumber` value, which is where some motherboard manufacturers choose to store their serial number.

The code retrieves the serial number value as a string, which can be output to the console. Note that not all motherboards will have a `BaseBoardSerialNumber` value in the registry, and some manufacturers may store the serial number value in a different location or format.

