One way to achieve this is by using a boolean flag to track if the loop should continue or not. Here is an example implementation:

```csharp
private bool continueLoop = true;

private async Task StartLoop(Message message)
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100 && continueLoop; i++)
    {
        // Do your loop logic here...
        await Task.Delay(1000);
    }

    // Send a message to notify loop has stopped
    await botClient.SendTextMessageAsync(message.Chat.Id, "Loop stopped.");
}

private async Task StopLoop(Message message)
{
    continueLoop = false;
    await botClient.SendTextMessageAsync(message.Chat.Id, "Loop will stop at the end of the current iteration.");
}

// Register your handlers for the bot prefixes
botClient.OnMessage += async (sender, e) =>
{
    if (e.Message.Text.StartsWith("/startloop"))
    {
        await StartLoop(e.Message);
    }
    else if (e.Message.Text.StartsWith("/stoploop"))
    {
        await StopLoop(e.Message);
    }
};
```

In this implementation, the `StartLoop` method contains the for loop, and it will keep running while the `continueLoop` variable is true. The `StopLoop` method simply sets this variable to false to stop the loop at the end of the current iteration. 

You can initialize the `continueLoop` variable to true at the beginning of your bot program, so that the loop will run by default.

One way to fix this issue is to run the for loop in a separate thread and check the value of a shared variable to determine whether the loop should continue or not. Here is an example implementation:

```csharp
private bool continueLoop = true;
private Thread loopThread;

private async Task StartLoop(Message message)
{
    loopThread = new Thread(() =>
    {
        for (int i = 0; i < 100 && continueLoop; i++)
        {
            // Do your loop logic here...
            Thread.Sleep(1000);
        }

        // Send a message to notify loop has stopped (to the chat of the message)
        botClient.SendTextMessageAsync(message.Chat.Id, "Loop stopped.").Wait();
    });
    loopThread.Start();
}

private async Task StopLoop(Message message)
{
    continueLoop = false;
    if (loopThread != null && loopThread.IsAlive)
    {
        loopThread.Join();
