To create a trait function that is protected from anyone outside the trait itself in PHP, you can use the `protected` keyword before the function name. Here is an example:

```
trait MyTrait {
    protected function myProtectedFunction() {
        // this function can only be accessed within the trait
    }
}
```

With this code, the `myProtectedFunction()` function can only be accessed from within the `MyTrait` trait. When you use the trait in a class, this function will not be accessible outside of the trait.

```
class MyClass {
    use MyTrait;

    public function someMethod() {
        $this->myProtectedFunction(); // this will work because it is called from within the trait
    }
}

$obj = new MyClass();
$obj->myProtectedFunction(); // this will not work because it is called from outside the trait
```

If you want to limit access to a function within a trait exclusively, even from classes that use the trait, you can define the function as `private` rather than `protected`.

A `private` function can only be accessed within the class or trait that declares it and cannot be accessed by any other class, even if that class uses the trait.

Here's an example:

```
trait MyTrait {
    private function myPrivateFunction() {
        // this function can only be accessed within the trait
    }

    public function doSomething() {
        // we can call myPrivateFunction() within the trait
        $this->myPrivateFunction();
    }
}

class MyClass {
    use MyTrait;

    public function doSomethingElse() {
        // this will cause a fatal error because myPrivateFunction() is private
        $this->myPrivateFunction();
    }
}

$obj = new MyClass();
$obj->doSomething(); // this will work as expected
$obj->doSomethingElse(); // this will cause a fatal error
```

Note that you should be careful when hiding functionality like this with `private` methods, as it can make your code harder to extend and test. Consider your use case carefully before deciding to make it completely inaccessible.

