To create a React Native switch, you can use the `Switch` component from the `react-native` library. Here's an example that shows how to create a basic switch with an `onValueChange` handler:

```javascript
import React, { useState } from 'react';
import { View, Switch } from 'react-native';

const MySwitch = () => {
  const [isEnabled, setIsEnabled] = useState(false);

  const toggleSwitch = () => {
    setIsEnabled(previousState => !previousState);
  };

  return (
    <View>
      <Switch
        trackColor={{ false: '#767577', true: '#81b0ff' }}
        thumbColor={isEnabled ? '#f5dd4b' : '#f4f3f4'}
        ios_backgroundColor="#3e3e3e"
        onValueChange={toggleSwitch}
        value={isEnabled}
      />
    </View>
  );
};

export default MySwitch;
```

In this example, we use the `useState` hook to keep track of the switch state. We also define a `toggleSwitch` function that is called when the switch is pressed. This function simply sets the state to its opposite value using the `previousState` parameter.

The `Switch` component takes several props:

- `trackColor`: object that defines the switch colors when it's off (`false`) or on (`true`).
- `thumbColor`: color for the switch thumb when it's off (`false`) or on (`true`).
- `ios_backgroundColor`: background color for the switch when running on iOS.
- `onValueChange`: handler function that is called when the switch is pressed.
- `value`: current switch state that is used to determine the switch position.

Tailwind CSS allows you to create custom CSS classes by utilizing its configuration file, `tailwind.config.js`. By utilizing this file, you can group multiple utility classes together under one custom class name. This way, you won't have to write long list of classes in your HTML.

Here's an example of how to create a custom class in `tailwind.config.js`:

```js
module.exports = {
  theme: {
    extend: {},
  },
  variants: {},
  plugins: [],
  corePlugins: {},
  purge: false,

  // Add your custom class configurations here
  plugin: function (context) {
    context.addBase({
      '.my-custom-class': {
        display: 'flex',
        alignItems: 'center',
        justifyContent: 'center',
        backgroundColor: 'red',
        fontSize: '1.5rem',
      },
    });
  },
};
```

Now, you can use `my-custom-class` in your HTML without needing the long list of utility classes.

```html
<div class="my-custom-class">
  Hello, world!
</div>
```

If you still feel that Tailwind is too verbose or not the right fit, you can explore other CSS frameworks such as Bootstrap (https://getbootstrap.com/) or Bulma (https://bulma.io/) which provide pre-built UI components with minimalistic class names.

You can use the "Headless UI" and "React-Datepicker" together with Tailwind CSS to create a customizable date and time picker for your project. Here's how you integrate them in a React project:

1. Install both Headless UI and React-DatePicker:

```bash
npm install @headlessui/react react-datepicker
```

2. Import Tailwind CSS and the required CSS for React-DatePicker in your project:

```javascript
// src/index.js or src/App.js
import 'tailwindcss/tailwind.css';
import 'react-datepicker/dist/react-datepicker.css';
```

3. Implement a custom date and time picker using Headless UI and React-DatePicker:

```jsx
// src/CustomDatePicker.js
import React, { useState, useEffect, useRef } from 'react';
import { Transition } from '@headlessui/react';
import DatePicker from 'react-datepicker';
import { format } from 'date-fns';

function CustomDatePicker() {
  const [selectedDate, setSelectedDate] = useState(null);
  const [isOpen, setIsOpen] = useState(false);

  const datePickerRef = useRef();

  useEffect(() => {
    const handleClickOutside = (event) => {
      if (datePickerRef.current && !datePickerRef.current.contains(event.target)) {
        setIsOpen(false);
      }
    };

    document.addEventListener('mousedown', handleClickOutside);
    return () => {
      document.removeEventListener('mousedown', handleClickOutside);
    };
  }, [datePickerRef]);

  return (
    <div className="relative w-64" ref={datePickerRef}>
      <input
        type="text"
        readOnly
        value={selectedDate ? format(selectedDate, 'Pp') : ''}
        onClick={() => setIsOpen(!isOpen)}
        className="block w-full py-2 pl-3 pr-10 border border-gray-300 rounded-md focus:outline-none focus:border-indigo-500 sm:text-sm"
        placeholder="Select a date and time"
      />
      <Transition
        show={isOpen}
        enter="transition ease-out duration-200"
        enterFrom="transform opacity-0 scale-95"
        enterTo="transform opacity-100 scale-100"
        leave="transition ease-in duration-100"
        leaveFrom="transform opacity-100 scale-100"
        leaveTo="transform opacity-0 scale-95"
      >
        <div className="absolute z-10 mt-1 w-full bg-white rounded-md shadow-lg">
          <DatePicker
            selected={selectedDate}
            onChange={(date) => setSelectedDate(date)}
            showTimeSelect
            timeIntervals={15}
            inline
            calendarClassName="rounded-md border border-gray-200 p-2"
          />
        </div>
      </Transition>
    </div>
  );
}

export default CustomDatePicker;
```

4. Use the `CustomDatePicker` component in your application:

```jsx
// src/App.js
import React from 'react';
import CustomDatePicker from './CustomDatePicker';

function App() {
  return (
    <div className="min-h-screen bg-gray-100 p-8">
      <div className="max-w-md mx-auto">
        <CustomDatePicker />
      </div>
    </div>
  );
}

export default App;
```

This example creates a custom date and time picker styled with Tailwind CSS that uses Headless UI for managing the visibility of the picker and React-DatePicker for date and time selection.

To prevent Bootstrap SCSS from being compiled every time you run `yarn run dev-server`, you can configure Webpack to output the compiled CSS from Bootstrap to a separate CSS file. Then, you can include that file in your HTML without having to compile it during development.

Here are the steps to do this:

1. Install `mini-css-extract-plugin` to generate the separate CSS file:

```bash
yarn add -D mini-css-extract-plugin
```

2. In your `webpack.config.js`, import the `MiniCssExtractPlugin` and configure it alongside the SCSS loader:

```javascript
// webpack.config.js
const MiniCssExtractPlugin = require('mini-css-extract-plugin');

// Other Webpack configurations...

module.exports = {
  // Other configurations...

  module: {
    rules: [
      // Other rules...

      // SCSS Loader
      {
        test: /\.scss$/,
        use: [
          process.env.NODE_ENV === 'production'
            ? MiniCssExtractPlugin.loader
            : 'style-loader',
          'css-loader',
          'sass-loader',
        ],
      },
    ],
  },

  plugins: [
    // Other plugins...

    // Add the MiniCssExtractPlugin for production builds
    ...(process.env.NODE_ENV === 'production' ? [new MiniCssExtractPlugin()] : []),
  ],
};
```

3. Create a new SCSS file, for example, `bootstrap.scss`, and import Bootstrap SCSS:

```scss
// bootstrap.scss
@import '~bootstrap/scss/bootstrap';
```

4. Instead of importing the `bootstrap.scss` file in your JavaScript entry point, compile it to a separate CSS file:

```bash
# Run this command in production mode:
yarn run encore production

# This will generate the compiled Bootstrap CSS file in the 'public/build' folder
```

5. Include the compiled CSS file in your HTML:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
  <head>
    <!-- Other metadata -->

    <!-- Include the compiled Bootstrap CSS -->
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/build/bootstrap.css" />
  </head>
  <body>
    <!-- Your app markup -->

    <!-- Include your app's JavaScript bundle -->
    <script src="/build/app.js"></script>
  </body>
</html>
```

Now, when you run `yarn run dev-server`, it won't compile the Bootstrap SCSS every time. The compiled CSS file will be loaded separately from your HTML file.

Note that you'll need to run `yarn run encore production` whenever you make changes to the Bootstrap SCSS to update the compiled CSS file.

