It is not possible to directly convert a canvas to an image. However, you can create a new image element and use the `toDataURL()` method of the canvas to set the `src` attribute of the image element. Here's an example:

```javascript
// Get the canvas and create a new image element
var $canvas = $("#population-chart");
var $img = $("<img>");

// Wait for the chart to finish rendering
$canvas.on("chart:render", function () {
  // Set the image source to the canvas data URL
  $img.attr("src", $canvas[0].toDataURL("image/png"));

  // Append the image element to the container and remove the canvas
  $canvas.parent().append($img);
  $canvas.remove();
});

// Same as before, render the chart using Chart.js and trigger the event
new Chart($canvas[0], {
  // Chart configuration
  options: {
    responsive: true,
    // Other options...
  },
  data: {
    // Chart data...
  }
}).render().then(function () {
  $canvas.trigger("chart:render");
});
```

Note that you need to wait for the chart to finish rendering before converting it to an image. This is accomplished by creating a custom event (`chart:render`) that is triggered when the chart finishes rendering, and then listening to this event to perform the conversion. Also, keep in mind that when converting a canvas to an image, any interactivity or animations are lost, so this approach is best suited for static charts.

