```rust
extern crate proc_macro;
use proc_macro::TokenStream;
use syn::parse::{Parse, ParseStream};

// This is a dummy syntax structure to try parsing the input tokens.
struct Dummy;

impl Parse for Dummy {
    fn parse(input: ParseStream) -> syn::Result<Self> {
        input.parse::<syn::Expr>()?;
        Ok(Self)
    }
}

// Function to check if there is any syntax error in the input string.
fn check_syntax_error(stream: &str) -> bool {
    let tokens = match stream.parse::<TokenStream>() {
        Ok(tokens) => tokens,
        Err(_) => return true,
    };

    let result = syn::parse2::<Dummy>(tokens);
    match result {
        Ok(_) => false,
        Err(_) => true,
    }
}
```

In this version, the `check_syntax_error` function takes a `&str` and attempts to parse it into a `TokenStream`. If the parsing fails, it returns `true` (indicating there's a syntax error). If it succeeds, it continues to check for syntax errors in the tokens using the `Dummy` syntax structure and returns `false` if there are no errors, and `true` if there are errors.

