You would put the `window.addEventListener` code inside the `<script>` section in `App.svelte` and update the `vehicleData` using a store or a reactive declaration. Here's an example of how to do that using a reactive declaration:

App.svelte:

```html
<script>
  import RealEstateTable from './RealEstateTable.svelte';

  let vehicleData = [];

  window.addEventListener('message', (event) => {
    if (event.data.type === 'showUI') {
      $: vehicleData = event.data.vehicleData;
    }
  });

  function handleClose() {
    // Call close event handler
    vehicleData = [];
  }
</script>

<RealEstateTable {vehicleData} on:close={handleClose} />
```

Note that we've added the `$:` in front of `vehicleData = event.data.vehicleData;`. This is a reactive declaration, and it tells Svelte to re-run this line of code whenever any of its dependencies change (in this case, `event.data.vehicleData`). As a result, when the `vehicleData` is updated, the `RealEstateTable` component will be rerendered with the new data.

