There is not a built-in Java function to compare two DWG files as it is a proprietary file type for AutoCAD software. However, you could use a third-party library to read and compare the content of the DWG files. Here's an example using the Autodesk AutoCAD DWG processing API:

```java
import com.autodesk.autocad.database.*;
import java.io.*;

public class CompareDWG {
   public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {

      // Paths of the two DWG files to compare
      String path1 = "C:\\path\\to\\dwg1.dwg";
      String path2 = "C:\\path\\to\\dwg2.dwg";

      // Open the two DWG files
      Database db1 = new Database();
      FileInputStream fis1 = new FileInputStream(new File(path1));
      db1.readDwg(fis1, true);

      Database db2 = new Database();
      FileInputStream fis2 = new FileInputStream(new File(path2));
      db2.readDwg(fis2, true);

      // Compare the two DWG files
      boolean areEqual = Objects.deepEquals(db1, db2);

      // Print the comparison result
      if (areEqual) {
         System.out.println("The two DWG files are equal.");
      } else {
         System.out.println("The two DWG files are not equal.");
      }
   }
}
```

This code opens two DWG files using the AutoCAD database API, reads the content of the files, and then compares the contents of the two databases using the `Objects.deepEquals()` method. If the two files are equal, the code will print "The two DWG files are equal." Otherwise, it will print "The two DWG files are not equal." Note that you will need to have the AutoCAD DWG processing API installed and referenced in your project for this code to work.

