Solution 1: Using syn::parse_str

In this solution, we'll use syn::parse_str to parse the string as an arbitrary expression.

```rust
use proc_macro2::TokenStream;
use syn::Expr;

fn main() {
    let erroneous_expr_str = "unimplemented(;";
    tokens_from_str(erroneous_expr_str);
}

fn tokens_from_str(stream: &str) -> TokenStream {
    match syn::parse_str::<Expr>(stream) {
        Ok(expr) => quote::quote! { #expr },
        Err(_) => TokenStream::new(),
    }
}
```

Solution 2: Using syn::parse

In this solution, we'll use syn::parse to achieve the same result as in Solution 1.

```rust
use proc_macro2::TokenStream;
use syn::{Expr, parse::Parser};

fn main() {
    let erroneous_expr_str = "unimplemented(;";
    tokens_from_str(erroneous_expr_str);
}

fn tokens_from_str(stream: &str) -> TokenStream {
    Expr::parse(stream.into()).map_or_else(|_| TokenStream::new(), |expr| quote::quote! { #expr })
}
```

Solution 3: Try parsing as a Statement

In this solution, we'll try parsing the input string as a statement instead of an expression. If it succeeds, we'll convert it into a TokenStream.

